% Folk songs from A. H. Fox Strangways, The Music of Hindostan (Oxford:
% Clarendon Press, 1914), chapters I and II, "A Musical Diary": songs he
% took down from singers on his journey of 1910-11, with his English
% singing translations. Public domain everywhere (the author died in 1948;
% US publication 1914). Scan: https://archive.org/details/musicofhindostan00foxs
% (scan page = printed page + 48).
%
% A first reading from the scan, 2026-11-09, to be checked against the plates
% at the pages given: the words are certain, the rhythms nearly so, and the
% pitches were read from the engraving at high magnification, so a note may
% be a step out where the N: line says check. These tunes are diatonic and
% carry no %%tuning line; Fox Strangways's "Tonic" note is given in N:.
% The Garhwali songs (pp. 50-1) are in 5/8, which he barred as written here.

X:1
T:O give me those pretty eyes
T:Garhwali song, a dialogue
C:traditional (Garhwal)
O:India, Garhwal (Himalaya)
R:song
S:Fox Strangways, The Music of Hindostan (1914), Ex. 97, p. 51
N:Tonic G (G Dorian with the one-flat signature). He and she sing alternate verses. The metre changes from 3/4 to 2/4 at "make". Check: bars 3 and 4.
M:3/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=100
K:F
%%style drone
G2 B c B c | c c c2 B B |[M:2/4] A2 B c | c2 c B | A2 B B | G2 G z |]
w: O give me those pret-ty eyes, And I'll make you the gift in re-turn of my own, dear.
N:2 (She): And if I give you my pretty eyes, Other lovers 'll laugh and 'll leave me alone, dear.
N:3 (He): Then O let me borrow them, And keep them safe till you need them again, dear.
N:4 (She): For six months you shall borrow them; Promise truly you'll give me them back then again, dear.
N:5 (He): What word is this of a bargaining! I will keep your eyes and your heart and your soul, dear.

X:2
T:Mother, send to the dhobi
T:Garhwali song
C:traditional (Garhwal)
O:India, Garhwal (Himalaya)
R:song
S:Fox Strangways, The Music of Hindostan (1914), Ex. 98, p. 51
N:Tonic B, no signature. Three verses; the dhobi is the washerman. The first three semiquavers are a pickup and the last bar is short by the same amount. Check: the pitches of bar 2.
M:5/8
L:1/16
Q:1/8=180
K:C
%%style drone
B d2 | c2 B c A2 A B c2 | c2 B c A2 A |]
w: Mo-ther, send to the dho-bi to bring back my li-nen.
N:2: Never mind where I'm going if you'll send to the dhobi.
N:3: Oh I'm going to get me a new wife, pretty Hira.

X:3
T:O where will you go
T:Garhwali song, the answer
C:traditional (Garhwal)
O:India, Garhwal (Himalaya)
R:song
S:Fox Strangways, The Music of Hindostan (1914), Ex. 99, p. 51
N:Tonic G, one flat. The reply to Mother, send to the dhobi; the two semiquavers are a pickup. Check: the last bar.
M:5/8
L:1/16
Q:1/8=180
K:F
%%style drone
A c | B2 A B G2 A B c2 | B2 A c B2 A B |]
w: O where will you go if I get back your li-nen?
N:2: You must say where you go before I send to the dhobi.
N:3: Little Chhaila is yours, and will you marry another?

X:4
T:Rama's lullaby
T:Maratha lullaby
C:traditional (Maharashtra)
O:India, Maharashtra
R:lullaby
S:Fox Strangways, The Music of Hindostan (1914), Ex. 161, p. 66
N:Adagio, crotchet 80; sung to him by a Hindu lady in Calcutta. No signature; the tune lies between middle C and A. Four verses, each ending in the refrain Little babe, sleep upon my knee. Check: bars 4 to 6 and the refrain's second bar.
M:3/4
L:1/16
Q:1/4=80
K:C
%%style drone
C C | D4 G3 F D3 C | C4 z2 C C D2 F2 | G8 z2 F G | A4 F2 G3 F | G3 F D3 C C4 z2 |
w: In A-yo-dhya, the land of kings, It was Vish-nu's self That was born on the lap of Kau-shal-ya.
C C | D4 G3 F D2 C2 | D3 C C8 z2 |]
w: Lit-tle babe sleep up-on my knee!_
N:2: For the cradle is wrought of gold, And a Child therein, Of the whole world the Lord and Master, sleeps.
N:3: In the hand-hold of Kaushalya Is the cradle-cord, And the cord is the thread of Bodhana (knowledge).
N:4: And the Devis (goddesses) are strewing flowers From the highest heaven, And with each flower a blessing.

X:5
T:Chhaila
T:Garhwali farewell song
C:traditional (Garhwal)
O:India, Garhwal (Himalaya)
R:song
S:Fox Strangways, The Music of Hindostan (1914), Ex. 111, p. 54
N:Written in the bass clef in the book; two bars make the unit and the rhythm is 2+3+3+2, which Fox Strangways says no barring reproduces. Sung a man's voice down; sing it an octave up for children. Check: bar 2's "now" (a minim in the plate, read here as a crotchet) and the last bar's rest.
M:5/8
L:1/8
Q:1/8=160
K:Bb clef=bass
%%style drone
F, F, F,2 F, | F, G, A, G,2 | D,2 E, D, C, | D,3 E, D, | B,, G,, G,,3 | C, C, (3D,E,D, C, | C,2 B,, G,, C, | C,2 C, {E,}D,3/2 C,/2 | C,3 z2 |]
w: Must you go, my Chhai_-la, now, Leav-ing me a-lone, my_ dar_-ling! Tell me, Chhai-la_ how I shall_ live when you are_ gone?
N:2: Shall I live, my Chhaila, say, Like a water-mill, my darling! Standing all the day desolate upon a hill?

